Subject: Game controllers: a fixed built-in scheme next to the keyboard and
 mouse

The first game controller SDL knows drives the game, with no setup and
nothing in the config file (a DualShock 3 is "PS3 Controller", whatever
its raw button numbers). The keys, the mouse and a joystick configured in
trigger-rally.config keep working at the same time.

  R2 (analog), Cross     accelerate
  L2 (analog), Square    brake, reverse
  left stick (analog)    steer; the d-pad steers like the arrow keys
  R1, Circle             handbrake
  Triangle               recover the car
  R3                     back to the last checkpoint
  L1                     next camera
  Select                 show or hide the map
  right stick            look left and right
  Start                  pause; while paused, Circle leaves the race

The menus answer the pad: the d-pad or the left stick moves a focus
between the clickable widgets (the nearest one that way; it is the mouse's
highlight, so it shows the same), Cross presses it, Start presses the
screen's default ("race") or else the focus, Circle goes back (Escape,
and "No" on the quit and leave-event questions), L1/R1 page back and
forward (the arrow keys). On a new screen the focus starts on the default
widget, else the topmost; on the prev/next arrows it stays on the arrow.
The mouse pointer hides while the pad drives the menus and comes back
when the mouse moves. In the car choice the d-pad or stick picks the car,
Cross or Start takes it, Circle goes back. The pause screen lists the
scheme.

A controller plugged in later is opened when none is; an unplugged one is
closed and the next one present takes over.

The joystick events looked up sdl_joy by the event's "which", which is
the joystick's instance id, not its index (SDL3, under sdl2-compat,
counts instance ids from 1 among other objects): a DualShock's axis
events wrote past the end of sdl_joy. They now find the joystick by its
instance id and skip any they don't know.

Forwarded: not-needed

